the seaside town of Torquay has long  been a popular haven for the elderly  this is war berries nursing home where  carers are doing their utmost to protect  vulnerable residents from the spread of  coronavirus the communal areas are  eerily quiet people are largely staying  in isolation in their rooms for their  own safety there's a strict no visiting  policy for relatives like so many other  homes war berries is battling dire staff  and equipment shortages but even more  worrying the homes owner has told Sky  News he's been asked to take in patients  from hospitals who are actively  suffering from ovid 19 very good reason  for that  that would be importing death into care  homes homes have done all the right  things against all the odds to add  insult to injury by asking us to take  cope with 19 positive patients is asking  us to basically make out the suicide  note for people in care this is one of  our lovely ladies giving out the tea  trolley staff took these videos of  themselves trying to maintain some  semblance of normality but nothing is  normal now least of all end-of-life care  so we've made an isolation  Lucey Hall is head of care and shows us  where they looked after a resident  suffering from a high temperature the  failure to carry out testing in care  homes means they'll never know for sure  whether he had coronavirus or not  because of that relatives weren't able  to be present when the 91 year old  passed away Howard his hand he says you  don't want to go anyway I don't know he  said he didn't want to be alone right  now so I sat with him but because of the  circumstances how many were able to be  with him so we had to be that person and  try and give him some form of dignity  and peaceful time within the last  moments of his life and that was really  hard really hard  Lucy's commitment to residents in the  home means she's had to stay away from  her own family that's been made even  more difficult because her grandfather  who has Parkinson's went into hospital  after a fall Lucy says he was put on a  ward with coronavirus patients but was  then sent home after testing positive my  heart broke because how can you do that  to somebody who's elderly they've got  Parkinson's yes that they are old but I  felt personally and as a family we felt  that they were basically sending him  home to die as the seasons pass  coronavirus is expected to keep  society's most vulnerable in isolation  for some time to come they may be out of  sight but carers say the elderly and the  vulnerable must not be forgotten Lisa  Holland Sky News talking
 

